\b\f5\cf4 Eight: Resurrection
\par }\pard \s16\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 
\par Brook stood in the tran-station outside the Medical Center, amazed at the number of people gathered there, waiting for the second coming of Axen Moon.  Small pots of flowers lined the entry-way, many sporting handwritten notes.  Papers were plastered over
 the bulkhead \emdash  signs, letters, and children's paintings wishing Axen well.  A group of people stood watching the safety lock expectantly and talking quietly among themselves.
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 How things have changed.}{\f5\cf4 
  How indeed, and though nobody knew it, those changes were all Brook's doing, directly or indirectly.  It had been four weeks since the colony relocation, and opening the Medical Center had become a colony-wide priority.  The sooner it opened, the sooner
 the injured Elder could be brought out of suspended animation and treated for his injuries, and today was that day.
\par 
\par It had 
been many months since Axen Moon miraculously arrived back in Eden, unconscious, half-dead, riding in a stolen Scout, with his Savant computer Kraft broadcasting the incriminating news about the mining colony on every available data channel.  It had rattl
ed Eden's society to its very foundation and started a wave of change that continued today.
\par 
\par Shortly thereafter the colony had been forced to relocate, and when they'd put it back together, everything had been different.
\par 
\par Someone took his arm gently.  He st
arted as he turned and realized it was Echo.  She smiled as though nothing had happened between them, and they were two young lovers out for a stroll.  "I wondered if I might run into you here.  Have you been avoiding me?"  She clicked her tongue.  "You k
now, I had made arrangements for us to be on the same Evac Transport, but somebody changed those arrangements at the last minute."  She locked eyes with him.  "Curious thing, don't you think?  And then, I just never seem to see you anymore, you don't answ
er my calls, don't do me my little favors.  What's happened between us?"
\par 
\par "I've been busy.  We're setting up a colony site in record time here."
\par 
\par She glanced at the decorated bulkhead.  "Especially the Medical Center I see.  Amazing what a little public will can do.  I can't say that I'm glad to see the old fossil back, but I can't really complain."  A smile crept back onto her lips.  "With the new
 status quo, the Masters have gone mainstream.  More and more people are listening to what we have to say, and th
e authorities don't dare touch us."  She chuckled.  "As I said, amazing about public will and all."  She looked up at him.  "Doing anything later, lover?"
\par 
\par He stared straight ahead.  "Yes."
\par 
\par She looked down at her feet and shook her head.  "Pity.  Jacque isn't busy tonight."  She swayed from side to side.  "Jacque can be a lot of fun."
\par 
\par Brook ignored her, which seemed to make Echo angry.  "He killed your friend, you know!"
\par 
\par "Not so loud!"  He looked around nervously to see if anyone was listening, but they seemed to be safe.  "Look, why don't you and your terrorist buddies just go soak in the mainstream or something?"
\par 
\par She just frowned.  "Suit yourself."  She turned and started to walk away.  "I'll be in my quarters tonight, if you want to call me.  But don't wait too long."
\par 
\par }\pard \s16\qc\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 # # #
\par }\pard \s16\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 
\par Brook stepped between the two Volunteer Guards, dressed in their newly issued blue and gold-trim uniforms, and into the private room.  One of the guards peered inside and made eye contact with the occupant.
\par 
\par "It's fine; close the door."  Axen sat propped up in the med-station bed, an oxygen tube clipped into his nose.
\par 
\par "How do you feel?"
\par 
\par "Not bad for a man who\rquote s been shot, blasted, dosed with poisoned air from a damaged spacesuit, frozen, and defrosted.  Yourself?"
\par 
\par "They said you'd asked to see me."
\par 
\par "They said you've been checking on my condition every hour for the past two days."
\par 
\par "I have an investment in you."
\par 
\par Axen's eyes narrowed as he stud
ied Brook's face.  "You obviously risked your life and status to rescue me, and to liberate Kraft.  So why do I feel like we're not exactly old friends here?   You got me back as far as Eden, then pulled a disappearing act and made it look like I'd escape
d.  Why?"
\par 
\par "It was easier and more forgivable for you to steal a Scout and a Savant than me, especially if it knocked the wind out of the Council.  Never mind that it was impossible for you to steal those things, I knew people would believe it of you.  The 
impossibility only adds to your mystique."
\par 
\par "You made me a hero."
\par 
\par "That was an unintended side effect.  Mostly I was covering my own backside."
\par 
\par He grunted.  "I might have done the same, if I'd been clever enough to pull it off."  He leaned back into his pillow and closed his eyes for a moment, as though he were already tiring.  "I asked for you because nobody tells me anything, and there are ques
tions I can't even ask.  What the frag happened while I was on ice?"
\par 
\par Brook kept his face a blank mask.  "First
 we have to settle something.  I'm going to ask you a question, and whatever the answer is, I want the truth.  If you can't tell the truth, keep your mouth shut and we'll end it right here.  How you answer may not change what I do \emdash 
 you're a means to an end now \emdash  but I have to know before I can work with you."
\par 
\par Axen's brow wrinkled.  "Go ahead."
\par 
\par "Did you kill Della Ricca?"
\par 
\par Axen's mouth dropped open.  }{\i\f5\cf4 "What?}{\f5\cf4   Where did you get that idea?"
\par 
\par "From Echo Van Dozier, initially, but it made a lot of sense.  The 
window was sabotaged, and you were in the CC repeatedly, including just before it happened.  You didn't like my associating with her, and she didn't seem to care much for your hanging around the CC to visit Kraft."
\par 
\par "Poor reasons for killing somebody."  He sniffed.  "If I were that cold-blooded, Nguyen would have parted company with his head long before he had a chance to blow this planet wide open.  Killed her!  Great Maker."  He sniggered for a moment, then sobered
 and looked up at Brook.
\par 
\par "You do believe me, don't you?"
\par 
\par "Yes, I'd come to much the same conclusion, I just didn't want to believe the alternative."
\par 
\par "Which is?"
\par 
\par "That Echo killed Della, directly or otherwise."
\par 
\par "Van Dozier, the baby doctor?  First you tell me she's playing amateur detective, then you tell me she's a murderer."
\par 
\par "That baby doctor is the leader of the Masters, the terrorist group.  She used me to get to the Gene Bank, then destroyed it, intentionally, I now believe."
\par 
\par "You're sure of all this?"
\par 
\par "She's admitted most of it to my face.  She's insane, Axen, I see that now.  She believes we have all the genetic material here that humanity will ever need, all us 99}{\f5\fs16\cf4\up10 th}{\f5\cf4 
 percentile humans.  Clone us forever, and usher in the golden age."
\par 
\par "I assume you can prove all this.  Let's just have her arrested."
\par 
\par Brook pulled a straight-backed chair over next to the bed and sat down with a sigh.  "Things have changed while you were out of the picture, Axen.  }{\i\f5\cf4 Everything}{\f5\cf4 
 has changed.  When news of the illicit mining camp broke, the Council was disban
ded, several members put under arrest.  The chairman was cleared of any involvement.  They'd taken advantage of the distractions created by the disaster to run this without the chairman's knowledge.  The chairman's forming a tribunal to charge them formal
ly, and the VG are being organized into a real police force."
\par 
\par "This all sounds to the good.  So, have the new police arrest her."
\par 
\par "It's not that simple.  The existing power structures have been split and fragmented.  Some people see the Masters as heroic revolutionaries, visionaries.  Echo has gone public, selling her ideas as a kind of immortality."
\par 
\par "People are buying this?"
\par 
\par "Those who haven't bought into the idea of you as a new messiah and maybe some of those too.  Enough to make them politically untouchable at the moment.  They signed their handiwork when they hijacked the Gene Bank.  If they were going to be arrested, it 
would have already happened."
\par 
\par "Frag, this is bad."  He looked up.  "Where's Kraft?"
\par 
\par "Under protective guard in the next room.  I don't think they wanted you wearing yourself out talking to the computer."  He chuckled.  "Kraft is almost as much a hero as you are.  The good news is, you're probably politically untouchable too.  I don't thi
nk you have to worry about any more assassins.  Nobody wants to make you a martyr."
\par 
\par Axen frowned.  "How did you know about that?"
\par 
\par "You babbled halfway back to Eden.  You'd be surprised what I know."  Actually, the attempt on Axen's life was about all he'd heard, but he'd just let Axen stew anyway.
\par 
\par "You think these guards can be trusted?"
\par 
\par "The VG seem to be staying neutral, and no one faction has a clear advantage.  The chairman and his supporters are focused on survival and keeping the colony running.  You have a lot more power than you did before, but it\rquote 
s effectively canceled out, just like everyone else's."
\par 
\par "You've seen the Van Dozier woman lately?"
\par 
\par "A few days ago.  She was chiding me for hanging around the Med Center.  I told her to get lost."
\par 
\par "Did she?"
\par 
\par "She didn't give up, if that's what you mean."  He felt his jaw clinch.  "I still have feelings for her.  I know she's insane, but part of me just wants to fix her.  But I'm staying away from her.  Don't worry."
\par 
\par Axen grunted.  "To the contrary, that's exactly what I don't want you to do."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 "What?"
\par }{\f5\cf4 
\par "Be her friend, give her what she wants, within reason, tell her I'm a murderer, whatever it takes."
\par 
\par "Axen, I can't."
\par 
\par "You can, and you will.  We can't let this situation continue, and if things are as evenly balanced as you tell me, this is our only hope of gaining an advantage."
\par 
\par Brook said nothing.
\par 
\par "You'll do it."
\par 
\par Reluctantly, Brook nodded.  }{\i\f5\cf4 Ask me to saw off my right arm.  It would be easier.
